  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nSeasonal temperatures in the 50s and 60s (F) are forecast this \nafternoon under partly cloudy to mostly sunny skies. With this, \nbreezy conditions will continue with gusts in the teens to 20s \nmph, with a few gusts into the 30s mph around the wind prone \nfoothills and over far eastern Montana. Per radar and satellite \nimagery, isolated snow/rain showers have developed near and east \nof Rosebud County within the upper level cyclonic flow over \neastern Montana. Look for these snow/rain showers to gradually \ndecrease in coverage this evening. Temperatures tonight will be a \nfew degrees warmer than last night in the 20s and 30s (F). \n\nSunday through Tuesday, conditions will trend warmer and drier as\na ridge of high pressure nudges into the region. During this \ntime, look for daytime high temperatures over the lower elevations\nand plains to warm into the 60s and 70s Sunday, 70s to lower 80s \nMonday, and upper 70s and 80s Tuesday. With this, there is even a \nchance (10 to 30 percent) of seeing 90 degrees over portions of \neastern Montana (mainly along and near the Yellowstone River \nvalley) Tuesday. While cooler temperatures will remain in the \nmountains, daytime temperatures will be plenty warm enough to \nallow mountain snowmelt to resume. With this, overnight lows in \nthe mid- elevations (7000 to 9000 feet) may even stay near to \nabove freezing by Monday night, allowing a bit more efficient \nsnowmelt. While no flooding is expected, some rises on streams and\nrivers coming out of the mountains can be expected. For \nadditional information regarding current and forecast streamflows \nand stages, visit water.noaa.gov.\n\nWednesday looks to be a transition day as our next weather system\napproaches the area. Because of this, there remains some \nuncertainty in the high temperature forecast, but generally 60s \nand 70s are forecast west of Billings and 70s and 80s around and \neast of Billings. The chance of precipitation returns Wednesday as\nwell. With this, the divergent flow aloft combined with some \ninstability may (10 to 20 percent chance) allow for some \nthunderstorm development. The main issue with this is the best low\nlevel moisture fetch coming in from the southeast may lift too \nfar north into northern Montana and southern Saskatchewan. \n\nA complex weather system will then bring a pattern change to the \narea Thursday into Friday as it moves through our area in some \nfashion. At this point, the biggest question mark regarding this \nsystem is the track, and how the two pieces of energy (an upper \nlow moving in from the west and a shortwave trough to the north) \ninteract. Either way, a cold front passage Thursday looks to bring\ncooler temperatures back to the region. Depending on how the \nupper low evolves over our area, another push of even colder air \nis possible Friday. With this, rain changing over to snow is a \npossibility. Along with the cooler temperatures and precipitation \nchances, windy conditions look to return as well. Please stay up \nto date with the latest forecast for updates through the week. \nArends",
